# SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
#
# Configuration for fastcache-compile-node.service.
#
# Everything here is site-specific and has no defensible default, which is why
# the unit reads it from a file rather than carrying it. The service will not
# start until at least --scheduler and one --toolchain are set.
#
# Arguments are passed through the shell-less systemd expansion, so quote
# nothing and keep each on one line.

# The scheduler's --listen-dispatch endpoint, and the compilers this worker
# serves. Give --toolchain a plain compiler path: the worker computes the
# fingerprint itself, the same way its clients do. Repeat it per toolchain.
#
# The endpoint clients should use to reach this worker must be an address they
# can actually resolve -- the scheduler hands it to them verbatim, so a worker
# that advertises 127.0.0.1 is leased and then never answers.
#
#FASTCACHE_NODE_ARGS=--scheduler=build-cache.internal:6675 --advertise=worker-01.internal:6676 --toolchain=/usr/bin/g++ --toolchain=/usr/bin/clang++

# Add --requirepass=<secret> when the scheduler was started with one. Prefer a
# drop-in with a tighter mode over putting a secret in this file:
#
#     systemctl edit fastcache-compile-node

# Add --admin-listen=<port> to serve /metrics and /healthz. It is off unless
# asked for, and a bare port binds loopback -- a scrape surface on a public
# interface is a decision, not something you get by typing a port. Write
# --admin-listen=0.0.0.0:6677 when you mean the network.
#
# Worth setting even if nothing scrapes it: without /healthz a supervisor can
# tell that this process is alive but not that it is answering, which is exactly
# the state a wedged worker is in.
